('110.24.b.9.D',3,'An extended metaphor is...','["a comparison developed over several lines or paragraphs", "a sound effect", "a list of facts", "a one-word rhyme"]'::jsonb,0,'Extended = continued.','original'),
('110.24.b.9.D',4,'A writer compares life to a long road throughout an entire essay, mentioning detours, signposts and mile markers. This is a(n)...','["alliteration", "extended metaphor", "onomatopoeia", "oxymoron"]'::jsonb,1,'A comparison sustained across a text.','original'),
('110.24.b.9.E',3,'A story is told by two characters who see the same event differently. This uses...','["a single narrator", "an index", "multiple points of view", "a glossary"]'::jsonb,2,'Different perspectives.','original'),
('110.24.b.9.E',3,'A fire station that burns down on the day of a fire-safety fair is an example of...','["simile", "alliteration", "rhyme", "irony"]'::jsonb,3,'Outcome opposite of expectation.','original'),
('110.24.b.9.E',4,'A reader knows a character is walking into a trap, but the character does not. This is...','["dramatic irony", "verbal irony only", "situational irony only", "a flashback"]'::jsonb,0,'The audience knows more than the character.','original'),
('110.24.b.9.F',3,'Words like ''gloomy,'' ''shadowed'' and ''silent'' most likely create what mood?','["silly", "somber", "joyful", "energetic"]'::jsonb,1,'Word choice sets mood.','original'),
('110.24.b.9.F',4,'An author''s distinctive style, including sentence length and word choice, contributes to the author''s...','["index", "table of contents", "voice", "glossary"]'::jsonb,2,'Voice is the author''s personality in writing.','original'),
('110.24.b.9.F',3,'A narrator who calls a dull meeting ''a thrilling festival of paperwork'' uses what tone?','["fearful", "sincere", "formal", "sarcastic"]'::jsonb,3,'Words say the opposite of the meaning.','original'),
('110.24.b.9.G',3,'An analogy explains something unfamiliar by...','["comparing it to something familiar", "citing a source", "listing synonyms", "removing detail"]'::jsonb,0,'Analogy compares.','original'),
('110.24.b.9.G',4,'''Just as a seed needs sunlight to grow, a student needs encouragement to learn.'' This is an example of...','["a slippery slope", "analogy", "a statistic", "juxtaposition"]'::jsonb,1,'It parallels two relationships.','original'),
('110.24.b.9.G',3,'Placing a lavish party next to a scene of hunger to highlight inequality is...','["foreshadowing", "a pun", "juxtaposition", "alliteration"]'::jsonb,2,'Contrasting items side by side.','original'),
('110.24.b.10.B.i',3,'Which transition best shows contrast between two paragraphs?','["In addition,", "For example,", "Similarly,", "On the other hand,"]'::jsonb,3,'''On the other hand'' signals contrast.','original'),
('110.24.b.10.B.i',4,'A conclusion should...','["restate the thesis in a fresh way and leave a lasting thought", "repeat the introduction word for word", "list only sources", "introduce a new unrelated topic"]'::jsonb,0,'Conclusions close the argument.','original'),
('110.24.b.10.B.ii',3,'Which sentence best develops the idea that cities need green space?','["Parks are fine.", "Parks cool neighborhoods, reduce stress and give families places to gather.", "Some parks have grass.", "Cities exist."]'::jsonb,1,'Specific details develop depth.','original'),
('110.24.b.10.B.ii',4,'Which addition adds depth of thought to ''Reading improves writing''?','["Writing happens at desks.", "Books have covers.", "Exposure to varied sentences helps writers learn rhythm, vocabulary and structure.", "Reading is a verb."]'::jsonb,2,'Explains why.','original'),
('110.24.b.10.C',3,'Which revision improves word choice in ''The movie was good''?','["The movie was movie-ish.", "The movie was.", "The movie was fine fine.", "The movie was gripping and beautifully filmed."]'::jsonb,3,'Precise adjectives.','original'),
('110.24.b.10.C',4,'A paragraph jumps between ideas with no logical order. Which revision step fits?','["reorganize sentences so related ideas are grouped", "change the title", "add more adjectives", "fix commas only"]'::jsonb,0,'Revising for organization.','original'),
('110.24.b.10.C',3,'A writer combines two short choppy sentences into one smooth sentence. This improves...','["spelling only", "sentence variety and flow", "the byline", "the title"]'::jsonb,1,'Combining sentences adds variety.','original'),
('110.24.b.10.D.ii',3,'Which sentence is in the passive voice?','["The ball broke the window.", "The ball hit hard.", "The window was broken by the ball.", "We saw the ball."]'::jsonb,2,'The subject receives the action.','original'),
('110.24.b.10.D.ii',3,'Which revision changes ''The cake was eaten by the guests'' to active voice?','["The cake the guests eaten.", "Eaten by the guests was the cake.", "The cake was eaten.", "The guests ate the cake."]'::jsonb,3,'The doer is the subject.','original'),
('110.24.b.10.D.ii',4,'Which sentence keeps verb tense consistent?','["She walked to the park and played soccer.", "She walked to the park and plays soccer.", "She walks to the park and played soccer.", "She will walk to the park and played soccer."]'::jsonb,0,'Both verbs are past tense.','original'),
('110.24.b.10.D.iii',3,'In ''The box of pencils is on the shelf,'' what is the subject?','["is", "box", "pencils", "shelf"]'::jsonb,1,'The prepositional phrase does not determine agreement.','original'),
('110.24.b.10.D.iii',4,'Choose the correct verb: ''The teacher, along with her students, ____ going on the trip.''','["were", "are", "is", "have been"]'::jsonb,2,'The subject ''teacher'' is singular.','original'),
('110.24.b.10.D.iii',3,'Which is a prepositional phrase?','["the tall boy", "ran quickly", "and sang", "under the bridge"]'::jsonb,3,'Preposition plus object.','original'),
('110.24.b.10.D.iv',3,'Choose the correct pronoun: ''Neither of the girls left ____ umbrella.''','["her", "their", "our", "his or their"]'::jsonb,0,'''Neither'' is singular.','original'),
('110.24.b.10.D.iv',4,'Which sentence has a vague pronoun reference?','["Sam met Jack, and Sam was nervous.", "When Sam met Jack, he was nervous.", "Jack was nervous when he met Sam.", "Sam was nervous when he met Jack."]'::jsonb,1,'''He'' could refer to either person.','original'),
('110.24.b.10.D.iv',3,'Choose the correct pronoun: ''The committee made ____ decision.''','["they", "his or hers and theirs", "its", "their own selves"]'::jsonb,2,'Collective noun as one unit.','original'),
('110.24.b.10.D.v',3,'Which small word is usually left lowercase in the middle of a title?','["Center", "Journey", "Earth", "of"]'::jsonb,3,'Short prepositions stay lowercase.','original'),
('110.24.b.10.D.v',2,'Which needs a capital letter?','["monday", "today", "morning", "week"]'::jsonb,0,'Days of the week are proper nouns.','original'),
('110.24.b.10.D.vi',3,'Which sentence uses a colon correctly?','["You will: need three things a pencil, paper and a ruler.", "You will need three things: a pencil, paper and a ruler.", "You will need: three things a pencil, paper and a ruler.", "You will need three: things a pencil, paper and a ruler."]'::jsonb,1,'A colon follows a complete clause introducing a list.','original'),
('110.24.b.10.D.vi',4,'Which sentence correctly uses parentheses?','["The festival held) every June (draws thousands.", "The festival ()held every June draws thousands.", "The festival (held every June) draws thousands.", "The festival (held every June draws thousands."]'::jsonb,2,'Parentheses enclose extra information.','original'),
('110.24.b.10.D.vi',3,'Which sentence correctly punctuates a nonrestrictive clause?','["My, brother who plays tuba joined the band.", "My brother who plays tuba, joined the band.", "My brother, who plays tuba joined the band.", "My brother, who plays tuba, joined the band."]'::jsonb,3,'Commas set off nonessential clauses.','original'),
('110.24.b.10.D.vii',3,'Choose the correct word: ''The dog wagged ____ tail.''','["its", "its''", "it is", "it''s"]'::jsonb,0,'''Its'' shows possession.','original'),
('110.24.b.10.D.vii',3,'Choose the correct word: ''Put the books over ____.''','["theyre", "there", "they''re", "their"]'::jsonb,1,'''There'' indicates place.','original'),
('110.24.b.10.D.vii',3,'Choose the correct word: ''The new law will ____ many families.''','["afect", "effect", "affect", "effects-on"]'::jsonb,2,'Affect is a verb meaning to influence.','original'),
('110.24.b.12.E',3,'A photograph taken during a 1963 march is a...','["secondary source", "encyclopedia article", "textbook", "primary source"]'::jsonb,3,'Created at the time.','original'),
('110.24.b.12.E',4,'A magazine article written last year analyzing a 1920s trial is a...','["secondary source", "diary", "court record", "primary source"]'::jsonb,0,'It analyzes events from later.','original'),
('110.24.b.12.G',3,'Which is an example of plagiarism?','["paraphrasing and citing", "using another author''s sentences without credit", "summarizing and citing", "quoting with quotation marks and credit"]'::jsonb,1,'Uncredited copying.','original'),
('110.24.b.12.G',4,'Which is acceptable paraphrasing?','["copying and pasting", "using the author''s exact wording without credit", "restating the idea in your own words and citing the source", "changing two words and not citing"]'::jsonb,2,'Original wording plus credit.','original'),
('110.24.b.12.G',3,'Why is citing sources important?','["it hides the evidence", "it shortens the essay", "it adds rhymes", "it gives credit and shows where information came from"]'::jsonb,3,'Citations are ethical.','original'),
('110.24.b.12.H.i',3,'Which clue suggests a source is less credible?','["no author or date and no listed sources", "a named expert author", "cited research", "a recent date"]'::jsonb,0,'Missing information reduces credibility.','original'),
('110.24.b.12.H.i',4,'A pamphlet from a company reports only positive reviews of its product. This is an example of...','["a primary source", "bias by omission", "a balanced report", "an unbiased study"]'::jsonb,1,'Negative information is left out.','original'),
('110.24.b.12.H.i',3,'A reader checks whether an article''s claims match other reliable sources. This evaluates...','["the page color", "the font", "reliability", "spelling only"]'::jsonb,2,'Corroboration shows reliability.','original'),
('110.24.b.12.H.ii',3,'''Everybody is switching to this brand'' is an example of...','["a statistic", "a primary source", "a definition", "a bandwagon appeal"]'::jsonb,3,'It relies on popularity.','original'),
('110.24.b.12.H.ii',4,'An ad repeats ''Fast, fast, fast!'' in every sentence instead of giving evidence. This faulty reasoning is...','["repetition", "a citation", "a counterargument", "an analogy"]'::jsonb,0,'Repetition replaces proof.','original'),
('110.24.b.12.H.ii',4,'Calling a proposal ''a reckless scheme by selfish politicians'' uses...','["a quotation", "loaded language", "a statistic", "neutral language"]'::jsonb,1,'Emotional words slant the message.','original')
) as v(code, diff, prompt, choices, ans, expl, src)
join public.teks_items i on i.code = v.code and i.is_standard
join public.teks_documents d on d.id = i.document_id and d.subject = 'English Language Arts and Reading' and d.language = 'en'
on conflict (fingerprint) do nothing;
